<% content_for :title, "Type chart" %>
<h1>Type chart</h1>
<p class="prose">
  Every spell and skill that does damage can have a type, and every monster has one. A move against a monster's type can be
  <strong>super effective</strong> (double damage), <strong>not very effective</strong> (half) or have <strong>no effect</strong>.
  The basic Attack has no type, so it always lands as it is. A monster can break the chart where its Bestiary entry says so.
  Some types also shrug off a status: poison and steel can't be poisoned, electric can't be paralysed.
</p>
<div class="window type-chart-wrap">
  <table class="type-chart">
    <caption class="visually-hidden">Attacking type down the side, defending type along the top</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col"><span class="muted">Move ↓ · Target →</span></th>
        <% Battle::TYPES.each do |defending| %><th scope="col"><%= type_tag(defending) %></th><% end %>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <% Battle::TYPES.each do |attacking| %>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><%= type_tag(attacking) %></th>
          <% Battle::TYPES.each do |defending| %>
            <% percent = Battle::Types::CHART.fetch(attacking).fetch(defending, 100) %>
            <% label, klass = { 200 => [ "×2", "is-x2" ], 50 => [ "½", "is-half" ], 0 => [ "0", "is-zero" ] }.fetch(percent, [ "", "" ]) %>
            <td class="<%= klass %>" title="<%= term(attacking) %> against <%= term(defending) %>: <%= percent %>%"><%= label %></td>
          <% end %>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
